From 0f68bd5068c9447f6da532576dda873927f0b122 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 11:38:20 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(turning): read the lathe axis by consensus when a large
 keyway fails the plain gate

The axis fit claimed an axis only when 90 % of the answering surface met
the plain least-squares line, and the #71 robust pass ran only after
that gate. A large keyway or flat failed it first: a 10 mm x 20 mm
keyway 6 mm deep on a D20 shaft drags the plain line 0.57 mm off,
twice the sagitta band, and a 50 mm keyway sets aside 17 % of the
surface. Both were refused as "share no rotation axis".

When no candidate passes the plain gate, motion's new axis_consensus
pass fits each candidate direction again by consensus. It keeps the
facets whose normal lines meet the line within a MAD-scaled cut that is
at least halved each round down to the sagitta band, and it holds the
candidate's direction so that a pencil of prism faces cannot swing it.
It then reads the gate about the consensus line at 75 % of the
answering area (CONSENSUS_SHARE), with the coarseness and pencil gates
unchanged, and hands the line to the robust reweighting at that share.
Parts that pass the plain gate never reach the fallback, so their fits
and the goldens are unchanged. An off-axis lump carrying 30 % of the
surface, a box, and #208's plate with a boss are still refused by name.

The spindle law's refusal text moves into `refusal()` so that the
untrusted (biased-fit) message is pinned by unit tests.

feat, not fix: the old refusal was safe and by design, and this turns
parts that could not be turned before.

Fixes #113

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
